This module is an introduction to the core architectural components of Azure. It teaches about the physical organization of 
Azure: datacenters, availability zones, and regions; it also teaches about the organizational structure of Azure: resources and resource 
groups, subscriptions, and management groups.

The module begins with the definition of Microsoft Azure. 
Azure is a cloud service provider that provides ever expanding cloud services that meet your current and future business needs.

To create and use azure services, you need a subscription. A subscription is automatically created when you created an azure account,
which is needed to access a subscription. You can create multiple subscriptions for different organizational departments such as
HR subscription, I.T subscription, etc.
After creating a subscription, you can create resources and group them within resource groups. 

You can navigate azure through the web portal, or the cloudshell which is command line interface based via bash or powershell.

Azure physical infrastructure is the physical data centres where azure services are deployed. Datacenters are grouped into azure regions 
and availability zones to provide resiliency and reliability for customers. 

Regions are a group of availability zones connected with fast high speed fibre connections for low latency.
Availability zones are datacentres within a region.
Region pairs are two regions 300miles or more apart from each other paired with high speed fibre cables. 
Sovereign regions are datacentres that are isolated from the general public for compliance and regulatory requirements. 

Management of resources in azure.
The hierachichal management of resources will help understand inheritance and who has the most contol over resources in an organisation.
A azure account holder has the most control over subscriptions and resources. 

Management groups help govern subscriptions and apply policies to them. Subscriptions are then used for billing and resource management
purposes. 
Resource groups help group resources. Everything in this order inherits from the parent manager. 
